# Discover abstractions from evidence

Do not build a generic engine because a second grid rule might appear. YAGNI: do not pay today’s complexity for a future that may never exist. AHA: avoid hasty abstractions. Duplicate a few lines until they share a concept, not just a shape. Factory DI is wiring, not a policy framework — see factory-functions.mdc.

```typescript
type Grid = { readonly breakPrice: number; readonly flipped: boolean };
type Predicate<Value> = (value: Value) => boolean;

// ❌ Framework for one flip rule. The next change negotiates Predicate, anyOf, and every consumer.
function anyOf<Value>(predicates: readonly Predicate<Value>[]): Predicate<Value> {
    return function combined(value: Value): boolean {
        return predicates.some(function matches(predicate: Predicate<Value>) {
            return predicate(value);
        });
    };
}

// ✅ Direct. Extract only after a second rule proves the same concept.
function shouldFlip(grid: Grid, currentPrice: number): boolean {
    const priceAtBreak = currentPrice >= grid.breakPrice;
    const gridStillOpen = !grid.flipped;
    return priceAtBreak && gridStillOpen;
}
```

A good abstraction removes a repeated concept. Keep change local: one flip rule must not drag a generic type and every consumer with it.
